## Features & fixes

- [x] Phase 0 — scaffold (configs, CI, plans, launch, VERSION/CHANGELOG)
- [x] Phase 1 — pure libs ported from useful with tests green (275 unit tests)
- [x] Phase 2 — paper design system (theme, global, Layout)
- [x] Phase 3 — vault storage (frontmatter, fs model, sqlite worker, prefs)
- [x] Phase 4 — the page (title, frontmatter, red rule, autosave) — CHECKPOINT A
- [x] Phase 5 — editor stack (3 faces, footnotes, diagrams, drawings, math)
- [x] Phase 6 — links, tags, search, the Ctrl+K palette, bulk ops — CHECKPOINT B
- [x] Phase 7 — export (MD/HTML/print), blank-page default, settings
- [x] Phase 8 — PWA (manifest, sw.js) + Playwright harness — CHECKPOINT C
- [x] Phase 9 — Tauri build, docs trees, README, release polish
- [x] UI rework (asked for mid-build): no sidebar, the book, the 8x11 page,
      blank-first, everything through Ctrl+K — decision 10 in the phased plan
- [x] 288 unit tests + 28 Playwright cases green against the production build
- [x] Second UI round: separate paper/desk/pen settings with procedural wood
      and leather desks, palette section headings, settings moved into the
      palette (the /settings/ route is gone), the sheet no longer spilling
      long notes onto the desk, titles committing on blur instead of per
      keystroke, and no alias kept when a new note is named for the first
      time. 33 Playwright cases.
- [x] Third UI round: seven papers (card, lamplight, plain, legal pad,
      blueprint, cyanotype, black) with an optional red rule; a grain size
      for the desk textures; click anywhere on the page to write; clickable
      breadcrumbs; aliases and arbitrary YAML (values, lists, nested maps)
      as editable frontmatter fields; a legible book; and tabs that hand the
      vault over instead of refusing to open. 303 unit tests, 41 Playwright
      cases.
- [x] Fourth round: the hand-off is gone — tabs queue on a Web Lock, the
      holder runs the sqlite worker and serves the others over a
      BroadcastChannel (owner/follower, src/lib/vault/tabs.ts), and each
      open note is its own Web Lock (src/lib/vault/noteLocks.ts): read-only
      when another tab writes it, promoted when that tab moves on and this
      one is visible, a per-tab blank page, bulk actions leaving held notes
      alone, the pool probe in the worker so a failed init cannot delete
      the vault; the Green bar paper (eight now); the tan leather desk
      retuned to render as #a6430a; docs and changelog cleaned of the
      hand-off. 323 unit tests in 20 files, 45 Playwright cases.

- [x] Vault export/import: one zip of the whole vault (folders, `.md` files
      and images) from Settings, and an additive import that never
      overwrites — a clash is filed alongside. 345 unit tests in 21 files,
      50 Playwright cases.

## For human

This section is for me to keep track of what I need to manually review, feel
free to add to this

- [ ] Review the paper palette in a real browser (tokens in src/styles/theme.css)
- [ ] Decide whether the Tauri vault should get a folder picker in 0.2.0
- [ ] Three bugs were fixed in code ported from useful and are worth carrying
      back upstream: Alt+0 never reached the editor for a real keypress; the
      footnote dialog closed on the keystroke that opened it; the editor was
      keyed on the note path so a rename rebuilt it mid-typing. See the
      CHANGELOG's Bug Fixes.
- [ ] Run `npm run tauri:build` once on this machine to produce an installer
      (cargo check passes; the full bundle has not been run)
- [ ] Decide what iOS gets. Safari suspends background tabs, so a follower
      there waits on a suspended owner — today that means one tab at a time
      on iOS, and the docs say so.
- [ ] Try the two-tab flow by hand in a real browser: open one note in two
      tabs, close the writer, watch the reader promote; close the owner tab
      and check the other picks the vault up; the blank page in a second tab.

## Per change
Required guidence to follow per change

- Check off changes as you go
  - Make sure the TODO is kept up to date so that if a usage limit is hit I'm
    not left in the dark
- if data model is updated
    - There are adequete tests for the system to ensure no data migration
      issues, and that the system works both from the UI and has unit tests
- Add Changes to the CHANGELOG.md
    - If a version exists and does not have a release date yet, keep adding to
      it, if there's no versions without a release date, then bump version
      (e.g. `v0.1.0 -> v0.2.0`) in the version file, and add a new main heading
      to CHANGELOG.md for it (e.g. `v0.2.0 (unreleased)`) and add the changes
      under there.
    - Be brief, 2-4 setnences per change, and if you need more, prompt for
      approval from the user
    - Add all changes under the following categories as sub-headings:
      - Features
      - Bug Fixes
      - Other
        - For things like
          - Documentation improvements
          - Refinements to existing systems that weren't bugs
          - anything else that doesn't fit in the other sections
